{
  "result": "The old library at the end of my street closed every evening at eight, but the librarian always pretended not to notice if someone remained tucked behind the tall shelves, still reading.\n\nI spent most of my sixteenth summer hiding there, not because I loved every book, but because the air inside felt cooler and quieter than anywhere else I knew.\n\nOutside, bicycles rattled past, buses sighed at the curb, and heat rose in visible waves from the pavement. Inside, dust drifted through slanting light, pages turned softly, and the clock above the desk ticked without urgency, as if time itself had agreed to slow down for anyone willing to sit still.\n\nI read atlases without planning journeys, biographies of people whose names I immediately forgot, and novels whose endings I sometimes skipped because I preferred the middle, where everything remained possible.\n\nThe librarian, Mrs. Alvarez, recommended books by placing them silently beside my elbow. She never asked what I thought afterward. She seemed to understand that some reading is not for discussion, but for shelter.\n\nYears later, living in another city, I still look for that particular quiet whenever life becomes too loud. I find fragments of it in early morning kitchens, in trains before dawn, in the pause between rain starting and people opening umbrellas.\n\nIt never lasts long, but it lasts long enough to remind me that attention is a place we can build anywhere, simply by staying, listening, and turning one more page before going home.",
